data: {"event":"node_complete","node":{"id":"bon-n2","parent_id":"root","depth":1,"branch_index":1,"content":"One prime number is 7. To explain, a prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. Let's verify 7:\n\n1. It is greater than 1.\n2. Its only divisors are 1 and 7.\n3. It cannot be divided evenly by any other number.\n\nTo further illustrate, let's consider the numbers around 7:\n- 6 is not prime because it can be divided by 2 and 3.\n- 8 is not prime because it can be divided by 2 and 4.\n- 9 is not prime because it can be divided by 3.\n\nThus, 7 fits the definition of a prime number perfectly.","score":null,"status":"ok"}}

data: {"event":"node_complete","node":{"id":"bon-n3","parent_id":"root","depth":1,"branch_index":2,"content":"113","score":null,"status":"ok"}}

data: {"event":"node_complete","node":{"id":"bon-n4","parent_id":"root","depth":1,"branch_index":3,"content":"The number 2 is a prime number. Note that 2 is the only even prime number. For all other prime numbers, they are odd. Prime numbers are natural numbers greater than 1 that have no positive divisors other than 1 and themselves. As of my last update, there is no known largest prime number, and new larger prime numbers are discovered regularly.","score":null,"status":"ok"}}

data: {"event":"node_complete","node":{"id":"bon-n5","parent_id":"root","depth":1,"branch_index":4,"content":"2 is a prime number, representing the smallest and only even prime.","score":null,"status":"ok"}}

